WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.800
Okay.

00:00:01.800 --> 00:00:03.600
Cold open.

00:00:03.600 --> 00:00:16.761
If an AI agent has ever produced three screens of confident final answer, and then you found out the test was fake, the docs were stale, and the endpoint existed only in the agent's imagination, welcome.

00:00:16.761 --> 00:00:18.561
This is your video.

00:00:18.561 --> 00:00:21.997
Bubbles is not here to make AI sound more impressive.

00:00:21.997 --> 00:00:25.368
We already have enough impressive-sounding nonsense.

00:00:25.368 --> 00:00:28.869
Bubbles is here to make AI coding work leave receipts.

00:00:28.869 --> 00:00:38.640
So in this version we are going deeper: gates, quality agents, review agents, docs, spec freshness, retros, hotspots, recipes, and the super agent you ask when you do not know which clipboard to grab.

00:00:38.640 --> 00:00:44.557
Bubbles is a spec-driven AI agent orchestration system for VS Code Copilot Chat.

00:00:44.557 --> 00:00:48.773
Less fancy version: it is the work system around Copilot.

00:00:48.773 --> 00:00:52.027
Copilot can reason, write, search, and edit.

00:00:52.027 --> 00:00:59.645
Bubbles adds role separation, workflow modes, artifacts, gates, reviews, validation, and certification.

00:00:59.645 --> 00:01:14.216
It is the difference between asking one helpful person to be analyst, architect, tester, security reviewer, project manager, and release lead, versus giving the job to an organized crew with rules.

00:01:14.216 --> 00:01:16.016
Still AI.

00:01:16.016 --> 00:01:17.470
Fewer mystery puddles on the floor.

00:01:17.470 --> 00:01:22.494
Let's put the gates in the center, because they are the main character.

00:01:22.494 --> 00:01:27.446
Bubbles gates are not little badges you admire after the work is done.

00:01:27.446 --> 00:01:29.286
They are the permit booth.

00:01:29.286 --> 00:01:33.107
State transition guard checks whether status can move.

00:01:33.107 --> 00:01:39.546
All-scopes-done prevents a spec from pretending it is complete while scopes are still open.

00:01:39.546 --> 00:01:45.277
Per-DoD evidence says every checked item needs raw output, not a bedtime summary.

00:01:45.277 --> 00:01:49.805
Then the deeper gates start asking rude but necessary questions.

00:01:49.805 --> 00:01:54.333
Does the implementation contain stubs or hardcoded pretend data?

00:01:54.333 --> 00:01:57.164
Are endpoints wired into real consumers?

00:01:57.164 --> 00:02:01.409
Does the frontend call a backend route that actually exists?

00:02:01.409 --> 00:02:04.310
These gates are why Bubbles feels strict.

00:02:04.310 --> 00:02:06.110
And honestly, good.

00:02:06.110 --> 00:02:08.060
The repo needed adult supervision.

00:02:08.060 --> 00:02:13.856
The evidence rule is simple and slightly annoying, which is how you know it works.

00:02:13.856 --> 00:02:16.754
If a box is checked, where is the output?

00:02:16.754 --> 00:02:19.228
If a test passed, what command ran?

00:02:19.228 --> 00:02:24.459
If the bug is fixed, what adversarial case would fail if the bug returned?

00:02:24.459 --> 00:02:27.428
Bubbles is allergic to narrative evidence.

00:02:27.428 --> 00:02:29.761
It does not want, all good, boss.

00:02:29.761 --> 00:02:36.476
It wants the command, the exit code, the raw output, and the behavior that proves the scenario.

00:02:36.476 --> 00:02:39.303
That pressure changes how agents behave.

00:02:39.303 --> 00:02:45.382
They stop optimizing for sounding done and start optimizing for being verifiably done.

00:02:45.382 --> 00:02:47.182
Huge difference.

00:02:47.182 --> 00:02:48.982
Same keyboard.

00:02:48.982 --> 00:02:49.270
Better adult supervision.

00:02:49.270 --> 00:02:51.070
Now, the quality suite.

00:02:51.070 --> 00:02:59.370
This is the crew you call after the first implementation says it is done, but the floor still makes a suspicious noise.

00:02:59.370 --> 00:03:08.298
Chaos runs random and semi-random real-system usage to find brittle paths, timing bugs, race conditions, and workflow weirdness.

00:03:08.298 --> 00:03:16.877
Simplify reviews recent implementation and trims duplication, awkward abstractions, and code that got a little too excited.

00:03:16.877 --> 00:03:19.040
Harden goes deep on completion.

00:03:19.040 --> 00:03:23.434
Gaps hunts missing requirements and unfinished vertical slices.

00:03:23.434 --> 00:03:28.526
Security looks at threat models, auth, dependencies, and compliance risk.

00:03:28.526 --> 00:03:33.840
Put together, this group turns, seems fine, into, we actually kicked the tires.

00:03:33.840 --> 00:03:37.402
Chaos sounds theatrical, but the idea is practical.

00:03:37.402 --> 00:03:40.546
Users do not follow your perfect demo script.

00:03:40.546 --> 00:03:42.346
They click things twice.

00:03:42.346 --> 00:03:44.146
They go backward.

00:03:44.146 --> 00:03:46.032
They refresh during a save.

00:03:46.032 --> 00:03:49.734
They open two tabs and create a small weather system.

00:03:49.734 --> 00:03:57.487
The chaos agent runs stochastic and semi-random real-system behavior, both single actions and chained journeys.

00:03:57.487 --> 00:04:01.608
It is not randomness for comedy, although comedy may occur.

00:04:01.608 --> 00:04:05.450
It is pressure on the paths your scripted tests forgot.

00:04:05.450 --> 00:04:15.960
Use it after important flows: checkout, authentication, onboarding, scheduling, anything with state, timing, or a user who might reasonably behave like a person.

00:04:15.960 --> 00:04:25.923
Harden and gaps are the agents for that moment when everything looks complete, but your engineering instincts are making the old van door noise.

00:04:25.923 --> 00:04:34.433
Harden checks that tasks are actually complete, tests pass, policies were followed, and completion claims survive pressure.

00:04:34.433 --> 00:04:36.578
It is not there to be charming.

00:04:36.578 --> 00:04:42.182
Gaps compares requirements, design, code, tests, and docs to find missing pieces.

00:04:42.182 --> 00:04:52.145
Together they turn unease into a concrete list: missing scenario, weak test, stale spec, unwired endpoint, skipped validation, half-finished UI.

00:04:52.145 --> 00:04:56.780
You know, the stuff that likes to wait until production has snacks.

00:04:56.780 --> 00:04:58.580
Simplify is underrated.

00:04:58.580 --> 00:05:02.495
After a feature works, the code often has sprint brain.

00:05:02.495 --> 00:05:08.333
It is technically alive, but it may be wearing two jackets and carrying a toaster.

00:05:08.333 --> 00:05:18.656
The simplify agent reviews recently changed files for duplication, poor reuse, awkward abstractions, bloated functions, and inefficient patterns.

00:05:18.656 --> 00:05:22.429
Then it fixes the cleanup while the context is fresh.

00:05:22.429 --> 00:05:28.053
That matters because velocity is not just how fast you write the first version.

00:05:28.053 --> 00:05:32.111
It is how expensive the second and third versions become.

00:05:32.111 --> 00:05:36.860
Simplify is how you avoid making future-you pay rent on today's mess.

00:05:36.860 --> 00:05:41.654
Security in Bubbles is not a sticker you slap on after the demo.

00:05:41.654 --> 00:05:52.964
It is an agent with a job: threat modeling, dependency scanning, code security review, authentication checks, compliance concerns, and exposure review.

00:05:52.964 --> 00:06:01.878
Use it when you add endpoints, change auth, touch sensitive data, ship deployment surfaces, or create public workflows.

00:06:01.878 --> 00:06:05.548
Basically, use it before optimism gets expensive.

00:06:05.548 --> 00:06:11.391
The nice part is that security review becomes part of the same evidence trail.

00:06:11.391 --> 00:06:13.563
Not a separate foggy meeting.

00:06:13.563 --> 00:06:15.363
Not a doc nobody links.

00:06:15.363 --> 00:06:19.630
It sits with the work, where future reviewers can find it.

00:06:19.630 --> 00:06:24.020
The review agents matter because not every review is the same.

00:06:24.020 --> 00:06:26.357
Code-review is engineering-first.

00:06:26.357 --> 00:06:34.287
It looks for bugs, behavioral regressions, missing tests, risky code paths, and security-ish smells in the code.

00:06:34.287 --> 00:06:36.087
Findings first.

00:06:36.087 --> 00:06:37.887
Summary later.

00:06:37.887 --> 00:06:39.687
Good.

00:06:39.687 --> 00:06:41.487
System-review zooms out.

00:06:41.487 --> 00:06:48.638
It looks at product behavior, UX, runtime trust, simplification, and whether the whole thing coheres.

00:06:48.638 --> 00:06:57.135
Spec-review checks whether your specs are still reliable or if the code has drifted past them while nobody was watching.

00:06:57.135 --> 00:06:59.188
That separation is important.

00:06:59.188 --> 00:07:00.988
Use the right flashlight.

00:07:00.988 --> 00:07:07.361
If you bring a whole-system product review to a two-line parser bug, everybody gets tired.

00:07:07.361 --> 00:07:09.690
If you bring a code-only review to a broken user journey, you miss the point.

00:07:09.690 --> 00:07:12.909
Docs and specs are not paperwork in Bubbles.

00:07:12.909 --> 00:07:14.709
They are memory.

00:07:14.709 --> 00:07:20.416
They are the reason a long project does not become a campfire story with YAML.

00:07:20.416 --> 00:07:27.000
The docs agent keeps managed docs current, deduplicated, and aligned with execution truth.

00:07:27.000 --> 00:07:33.585
Spec-review checks whether specs are stale, obsolete, redundant, or drifted from the code.

00:07:33.585 --> 00:07:45.437
Analyst, design, plan, docs, and validation each own different surfaces so one agent does not casually rewrite the whole town charter because it got enthusiastic.

00:07:45.437 --> 00:07:48.217
This is how Bubbles keeps specs alive.

00:07:48.217 --> 00:07:50.017
Not perfect.

00:07:50.017 --> 00:07:50.660
Alive.

00:07:50.660 --> 00:07:50.660
That is the useful part.

00:07:50.660 --> 00:07:54.632
Retro is where Bubbles becomes a little more strategic.

00:07:54.632 --> 00:08:00.336
It is not just, how did everyone feel about the sprint, please choose an emoji.

00:08:00.336 --> 00:08:02.136
No.

00:08:02.136 --> 00:08:12.101
Retro looks at velocity metrics, gate health trends, shipping patterns, code hotspots, architectural coupling, and repeated failure areas.

00:08:12.101 --> 00:08:14.845
Hotspot analysis is especially useful.

00:08:14.845 --> 00:08:18.311
It asks where the repo keeps charging you extra.

00:08:18.311 --> 00:08:20.111
Which files churn?

00:08:20.111 --> 00:08:22.061
Which modules attract bugs?

00:08:22.061 --> 00:08:24.588
Which workflows keep failing gates?

00:08:24.588 --> 00:08:26.826
Which areas create review drag?

00:08:26.826 --> 00:08:31.664
That lets you improve the system, not just survive the next ticket.

00:08:31.664 --> 00:08:33.480
Very rude to the old chaos.

00:08:33.480 --> 00:08:33.480
Very helpful to the future.

00:08:33.480 --> 00:08:35.280
And then there is super.

00:08:35.280 --> 00:08:39.860
Super is the agent you ask when you do not know which agent to ask.

00:08:39.860 --> 00:08:41.660
Which is, frankly, humane.

00:08:41.660 --> 00:08:51.778
Super helps with framework operations, command generation, workflow guidance, agent selection, recipes, setup, upgrades, and general Bubbles advice.

00:08:51.778 --> 00:08:56.768
Recipes matter because repeated work should not be reinvented every time.

00:08:56.768 --> 00:09:08.253
Bugfix fastlane, validation loops, release packets, hardening passes, docs refreshes: these become patterns you can run, not folklore you remember if the moon is right.

00:09:08.253 --> 00:09:15.773
So if you are standing in the park office holding a broken workflow and three suspicious artifacts, ask super.

00:09:15.773 --> 00:09:18.120
It will point at the right clipboard.

00:09:18.120 --> 00:09:21.595
For everyday delivery, workflow is the workhorse.

00:09:21.595 --> 00:09:23.395
You describe the outcome.

00:09:23.395 --> 00:09:30.487
It resolves intent, picks owners, runs phases, and routes failed gates back to whoever owns the fix.

00:09:30.487 --> 00:09:36.728
This matters because diagnostic work should not accidentally become final certification.

00:09:36.728 --> 00:09:38.642
A review can find problems.

00:09:38.642 --> 00:09:40.983
A validator can certify evidence.

00:09:40.983 --> 00:09:43.181
An implementer can change code.

00:09:43.181 --> 00:09:48.571
Those are different jobs, and Bubbles tries very hard not to mix the badges.

00:09:48.571 --> 00:09:59.040
Use workflow when you want the whole trail: request, analysis, design if needed, scopes, implementation, tests, validation, docs, audit, and rework.

00:09:59.040 --> 00:10:01.873
For bigger work, use bigger controllers.

00:10:01.873 --> 00:10:05.486
Goal drives one outcome through a convergence loop.

00:10:05.486 --> 00:10:10.232
Sprint handles several goals with prioritization and a time budget.

00:10:10.232 --> 00:10:18.873
Releases prepares phase packets with vision, feature lists, actions, business context, carry-forward, and launch material.

00:10:18.873 --> 00:10:23.832
That is important when the work is no longer one bug or one component.

00:10:23.832 --> 00:10:31.907
Coordinators keep the larger campaign moving without asking one agent to cosplay as an entire software department.

00:10:31.907 --> 00:10:33.707
It is still Copilot.

00:10:33.707 --> 00:10:39.840
It just has traffic control now, which is nice because the old intersection was mostly hope.

00:10:39.840 --> 00:10:44.764
Actual developer pain: the UI calls a route from a dream sequence.

00:10:44.764 --> 00:10:47.599
The backend has almost the same route.

00:10:47.599 --> 00:10:49.399
The test mocked fetch.

00:10:49.399 --> 00:10:51.199
The demo smiled.

00:10:51.199 --> 00:10:56.794
Then the user clicked and the whole thing turned into interpretive logging.

00:10:56.794 --> 00:11:02.986
Bubbles attacks this with vertical slice completeness and integration completeness.

00:11:02.986 --> 00:11:06.418
Frontend API calls need real backend handlers.

00:11:06.418 --> 00:11:11.267
Backend endpoints need consumers or clear external documentation.

00:11:11.267 --> 00:11:13.281
Pages need to be reachable.

00:11:13.281 --> 00:11:15.370
Libraries need real imports.

00:11:15.370 --> 00:11:17.757
It sounds obvious because it is.

00:11:17.757 --> 00:11:21.170
And yet this bug has paid rent in almost every codebase.

00:11:21.170 --> 00:11:26.109
Another developer pain: the test passes by avoiding the fire.

00:11:26.109 --> 00:11:28.619
If redirected to login, return.

00:11:28.619 --> 00:11:31.210
If the control is missing, skip.

00:11:31.210 --> 00:11:36.796
If the data is weird, assert that something exists and call it a day.

00:11:36.796 --> 00:11:38.596
That is not testing.

00:11:38.596 --> 00:11:41.916
That is politely walking around the hole.

00:11:41.916 --> 00:11:45.964
Bubbles regression guard rules push the other way.

00:11:45.964 --> 00:11:49.202
Required scenarios cannot silently bail.

00:11:49.202 --> 00:11:51.793
Bug fixes need adversarial data.

00:11:51.793 --> 00:11:54.303
Live tests need the real stack.

00:11:54.303 --> 00:11:57.704
Persistence bugs need write, read, assert.

00:11:57.704 --> 00:12:00.052
Again, the goal is not fancy.

00:12:00.052 --> 00:12:04.505
The goal is that a broken feature makes the test angry.

00:12:04.505 --> 00:12:06.510
Groundbreaking, apparently.

00:12:06.510 --> 00:12:10.742
Long agent work has another problem: context wanders off.

00:12:10.742 --> 00:12:23.733
The chat was long, the feature was complicated, the session compacted, and now everybody is trying to remember whether mostly fixed meant fixed, or just emotionally available.

00:12:23.733 --> 00:12:30.340
Bubbles uses durable artifacts: spec, design, scopes, report, user validation, and state.

00:12:30.340 --> 00:12:34.200
Recap and handoff agents help move between sessions.

00:12:34.200 --> 00:12:38.952
State tracks execution without pretending certification is done.

00:12:38.952 --> 00:12:43.925
That lets the next session continue from evidence instead of vibes.

00:12:43.925 --> 00:12:47.860
The vibes may still attend, but they are not driving.

00:12:47.860 --> 00:12:49.919
Picture one checkout bug.

00:12:49.919 --> 00:12:52.884
Availability changes during payment.

00:12:52.884 --> 00:13:00.050
Without structure, an agent patches a handler, adds one happy-path test, and says done.

00:13:00.050 --> 00:13:03.262
This is how Friday learns martial arts.

00:13:03.262 --> 00:13:06.721
With Bubbles, the bug is reproduced first.

00:13:06.721 --> 00:13:10.015
Design defines the transaction boundary.

00:13:10.015 --> 00:13:11.992
Plan captures scenarios.

00:13:11.992 --> 00:13:13.887
Implement changes code.

00:13:13.887 --> 00:13:16.440
Test adds adversarial coverage.

00:13:16.440 --> 00:13:19.158
Chaos pokes real-system behavior.

00:13:19.158 --> 00:13:21.217
Harden checks completion.

00:13:21.217 --> 00:13:23.358
Security reviews exposure.

00:13:23.358 --> 00:13:25.158
Docs aligns truth.

00:13:25.158 --> 00:13:27.217
Validate checks evidence.

00:13:27.217 --> 00:13:29.017
Audit names risk.

00:13:29.017 --> 00:13:30.817
Same AI assistance.

00:13:30.817 --> 00:13:33.041
Much better operating loop.

00:13:33.041 --> 00:13:34.230
Less hoping.

00:13:34.230 --> 00:13:34.230
More receipts.

00:13:34.230 --> 00:13:36.030
The payoff is trust.

00:13:36.030 --> 00:13:37.830
Not blind trust.

00:13:37.830 --> 00:13:39.630
Earned trust.

00:13:39.630 --> 00:13:49.150
Bubbles lets agents take bigger swings because the work has structure, gates, evidence, reviews, and ownership boundaries.

00:13:49.150 --> 00:13:56.251
It reduces rework by catching fake done, stale specs, weak tests, and unwired code earlier.

00:13:56.251 --> 00:14:00.152
It gives reviewers a trail instead of a monologue.

00:14:00.152 --> 00:14:06.785
It lets teams use AI more aggressively without quietly lowering the bar for delivery.

00:14:06.785 --> 00:14:08.658
That is the whole trick.

00:14:08.658 --> 00:14:11.623
More trust per unit of generated work.

00:14:11.623 --> 00:14:13.090
Simple sentence.

00:14:13.090 --> 00:14:13.090
Hard habit.

00:14:13.090 --> 00:14:13.090
Very useful.

00:14:13.090 --> 00:14:15.939
So try Bubbles on one painful workflow.

00:14:15.939 --> 00:14:17.739
Not every repo.

00:14:17.739 --> 00:14:19.539
Not the whole company.

00:14:19.539 --> 00:14:21.339
One real problem.

00:14:21.339 --> 00:14:23.969
Pick the bug that keeps coming back.

00:14:23.969 --> 00:14:25.941
Pick the spec that drifted.

00:14:25.941 --> 00:14:30.032
Pick the release packet that always becomes archaeology.

00:14:30.032 --> 00:14:36.607
Install, bootstrap, ask super if you are unsure, or start with slash bubbles dot workflow.

00:14:36.607 --> 00:14:41.136
Then inspect the gates, artifacts, evidence, and review trail.

00:14:41.136 --> 00:14:48.442
If you want AI coding work that moves fast and still proves itself, Bubbles is worth a serious look.

00:14:48.442 --> 00:14:50.242
It is strict.

00:14:50.242 --> 00:14:52.042
It is practical.

00:14:52.042 --> 00:14:54.730
And it keeps just enough park-office energy to make the clipboard survivable.
